  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 215 PM CDT Mon Oct 26 2026\n\nWednesday through Monday:\n\nSouth winds into Wednesday will continue to bring warmer \ntemperatures into the area, with highs rising into the lower to \nperhaps middle 70s. It may be a little cooler near the lake if \nwinds turn southeast in the afternoon. More sunshine should \noccur as the area gets more into the warm sector. \n\nModels continue to show the cold front slowly shifting \nsoutheast across the area Wednesday night into Thursday morning,\nshifting out of the area during the afternoon. This feature \nshould help bring a period of showers (30 to 60 percent chances)\nto the area, mainly later Wednesday night into early Thursday \nmorning. A few thunderstorms may occur as well, with very weak \nelevated CAPE. Rainfall amounts should be fairly light. Highs \nshould still get into the upper 60s to lower 70s Thursday as the\nfront moves through, then cools down into the 40s Thursday \nnight with high pressure moving through the region.\n\n500 mb cluster analysis then shows an anomalous ridge axis \ngradually building across the Great Lakes region over the \nweekend into early next week. This should continue to favor \nmilder temperatures during this period, which are supported by \nensemble members. There should be chances (30 to 50 percent) for\nshowers at times Friday into Friday night, and 20 to 40 percent\nchances over the weekend, as low pressure passes by to the \nsouth of the area. Ensemble members are showing a trend for a \nwetter period this weekend, though there may be breaks at times.\n\nWood",
